{"key":"enterprise/T1552.003","id":"T1552.003","name":"Shell History","domain":"enterprise","level":"subtechnique","parent_id":"T1552","tactics":["credential-access"],"platforms":["Linux","macOS","Windows"],"environments":["cloud-hosted-endpoint","onprem"],"environment_basis":"project_taxonomy_from_upstream_platforms","summary":"Adversaries may search the command history on compromised systems for insecurely stored credentials. On Linux and macOS systems, shells such as Bash and Zsh keep track of the commands users type on the command-line with the \"history\" utility. Once a user logs out, the history is flushed to the user's history file. For each user, this file resides at the same location: for example, `~/.bash_history` or `~/.zsh_history`. Typically, these files…","source_url":"https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1552/003","source_stix_id":"attack-pattern--8187bd2a-866f-4457-9009-86b0ddedffa3","source_modified":"2025-10-24T17:49:02.375Z","source_object_version":"2.0","telemetry":{"status":"mapped","components":[{"id":"DC0055","name":"File Access","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/datacomponents/DC0055","stix_id":"x-mitre-data-component--235b7491-2d2b-4617-9a52-3c0783680f71","page":"telemetry/DC0055/"},{"id":"DC0039","name":"File Creation","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/datacomponents/DC0039","stix_id":"x-mitre-data-component--2b3bfe19-d59a-460d-93bb-2f546adc2d2c","page":"telemetry/DC0039/"},{"id":"DC0032","name":"Process Creation","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/datacomponents/DC0032","stix_id":"x-mitre-data-component--3d20385b-24ef-40e1-9f56-f39750379077","page":"telemetry/DC0032/"},{"id":"DC0034","name":"Process Metadata","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/datacomponents/DC0034","stix_id":"x-mitre-data-component--ee575f4a-2d4f-48f6-b18b-89067760adc1","page":"telemetry/DC0034/"}],"strategies":[{"id":"DET0385","name":"Detect Access and Parsing of .bash_history Files for Credential Harvesting","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0385","stix_id":"x-mitre-detection-strategy--653b555a-590f-40e4-9400-f14d0ed92252","relationship_id":"relationship--e34c3bf9-4bb9-4afb-87f4-ba83818f7ee8"}],"analytics":[{"id":"AN1085","name":"Analytic 1085","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0385#AN1085","guidance":"A process outside of interactive shell context reads ~/.bash_history directly (e.g., using cat, less, grep), often shortly after privilege escalation or user switch (su/sudo). This may be followed by credential scanning in memory or file writes to new locations.","platforms":["Linux"],"strategy_id":"DET0385"},{"id":"AN1086","name":"Analytic 1086","url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0385#AN1086","guidance":"A process or terminal command outside of standard shell utilities reads the user's .bash_history file. On macOS, unified logs or telemetry tools like EndpointSecurity (ESF) may observe file read APIs or terminal process lineage that shows non-user-initiated access.","platforms":["macOS"],"strategy_id":"DET0385"}],"log_sources":[{"component_id":"DC0055","component":"File Access","name":"auditd:SYSCALL","channel":"open/read access to ~/.bash_history","platforms":["Linux"],"analytic_id":"AN1085","source_url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0385#AN1085","validation":"upstream_definition_not_locally_validated","component_page":"telemetry/DC0055/"},{"component_id":"DC0032","component":"Process Creation","name":"auditd:EXECVE","channel":"cat|less|grep accessing .bash_history from a non-shell process","platforms":["Linux"],"analytic_id":"AN1085","source_url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0385#AN1085","validation":"upstream_definition_not_locally_validated","component_page":"telemetry/DC0032/"},{"component_id":"DC0039","component":"File Creation","name":"auditd:SYSCALL","channel":"write or create file after .bash_history access","platforms":["Linux"],"analytic_id":"AN1085","source_url":"https://attack.mitre.org/detectionstrategies/DET0385#AN1085","valid